(D9 — Discrepancy 9 repro: the rule at LANGUAGE.md:734 is enforced — a
 local read at top level IS a compile-time error — but the diagnostic
 points at the wrong place and gives a hint about a construct the program
 does not contain. The offending statement is the top-level
 `Print localsum.` on the LAST line; the caret lands on `Print localsum.`
 INSIDE the function (line 3), where the read is perfectly legal, and the
 hint says `localsum` "is declared only in some branches of an
 `if`/`otherwise`" — there is no `if` in the program.
 Deleting the last line compiles and prints 7, which is what identifies
 the last line as the real offender.
 The strongest pro-compiler reading: :706 promises only that this is a
 compile-time error, and it is. Nothing in the manual promises where the
 caret lands, so on the letter of the claim the compiler is correct and
 this is a diagnostic-quality defect rather than a wrong answer. It still
 sent this mapper looking for a scope bug inside the function body that
 was not there. Compare vox bug #46, "the diagnostic caret can land
 inside a comment".
